- Story & Context: Locals in Penzance still revere the freezing spring equinox when Mayumi Sato wheeled her solid-paulownia koto down the granite slipway onto the exposed tidal flats of Mount's Bay. The air was frigid and heavy with the reek of rotting kelp as battery-powered subwoofers rattled the bilges of grounded skiffs, while a quiet crowd of hundred hooded kids stood motionless in the mud. When an unexpected surge tide rushed in, soaking the stage boxes and spraying salt across the pick-ups, Sato refused to break tempo, leaning over the strings to chant: "Drown the carrier wave and let the silk string drift." The monitors held out just long enough to finish the set before dying with a beautiful, vaporous drone that became the defining myth of the coastal underground.
